WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.020
CS:GO's history features some of the
most scandalous and quite frankly

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:08.280
hilarious moments across the gaming
world and this is a tribute to some of

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.000
the game's most ridiculous shocking and
intriguing past.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.760
Considered one of the biggest controversies at the

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.000
time, Fnatic's infamous Overpass boost is
still debated to this day. Whether you

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.420
think the boost was an amazing play that
was engineered by the great CS:GO minds

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.600
at Fnatic or whether you think the
boost was completely illegal and

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.160
unnecessary, you have to admit it made
for a memorable Major.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.000
Down 13-3 after the first 16
rounds, Fnatic finally made their

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.300
three-man booth works as olofmeister
secured two quick kills on the

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.129
unsuspecting LDLC. Fnatic would continue
to use and abuse the boost round after

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:53.910
round for the rest of the game and it
would eventually win them that map over

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.989
LDLC. The big issue with this boost was
actually that when using the boost, some

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:01.380
textures would become invisible and that
was what was against tournament rules.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.800
"They realized by getting from social
media, that there is a texture bug. So a

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.799
texture transparency from that spot where
you can see almost down to the T-spawn, you

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:19.049
can see what's called tunnel and in the
rules that is not allowed." After a series

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:22.020
of back-and-forth between the teams and
the admins, it was eventually ruled that

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.100
the entire map would be replayed. After
the final decision, however, Fnatic

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.800
forfeited that match and LDLC would go
on to win their first Major championship.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.620
Although it's impossible to know exactly

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.160
when this lobby raider first appeared in
the private lobbies of professional CS:GO

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.060
players and tournaments around the world,
what we do know is the incredible amount

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.590
of craze this raider generated across the
CS:GO community. Appearing in the private

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:55.770
lobbies of even Valve sponsored events
such as ESL One: Katowice 2015 and ESL

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:00.180
One: Cologne 2015, this Raider gained
notoriety within the community at a

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.700
rapid pace.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:09.680
(Casting)

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.180
In no time, this chiken had a booming social media

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.050
following and so many fake accounts
imitating him that it would become

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:20.760
almost impossible to keep track of the
real one. This lobby raider gained so

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.110
much popularity so fast
that he even held an AMA with almost 500

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:29.730
comments and over 1600 upvotes. There are
several theories about the identity of

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.360
this anonymous lobby raider, with the
most popular being that this is a

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.620
character creation of the Youtuber
Failu, especially considering his video

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.730
contribution to the chiken craze. But I
guess there's no real way to tell for

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.870
sure. Like all things, however, this
character/meme would eventually fade

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:50.580
into obscurity, but we'll never forget
that time when no one was safe from the

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.560
chiken.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.620
Valve's Christmas gift to the CS:GO community, the

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:01.410
R8 update. The R8 update was, at the time,
probably the most game breaking update

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:06.720
in the history of CS:GO. Not only was the
R8 the most powerful pistol on release,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.830
it was hands down the most powerful gun
in the game. Although there were so many

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.519
things wrong about the R8 on release, the
damage model was hands-down the most

00:03:14.519 --> 00:03:19.680
broken. The gun had an extremely high
base damage of 115 which meant you could

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.120
kill an enemy with one shot to the chest. But not only that it came with a

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.260
million bugs. Not only could you waste
ammo during freeze time, you could hold

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.180
both the left and right mouse buttons to
get the accuracy of a channeled mouse one,  but

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.260
the speed of a mouse two shot. Not only
that, players could defuse the bomb and

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.630
right-click opponents at the exact same
time.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.910
Needless to say, the community
was outraged and tournament organizers

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:47.320
such as ESL refused to play on the new
update. With the entire wrath of the

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:52.750
community at its doors, Valve nerfed the
R8 in a blog post entitled damage

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.680
control. The nerfs were significant enough
that less and less players began using

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:02.020
the R8 over time, and although the R8 is
hardly ever used now, there was a point

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:06.220
in time when the R8 outdid every other
gun.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.100
The infamous Train bug, aka the bird boost. In December of 2014, Train was reintroduced to CS:GO and

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.460
it came with a complete facelift. This
was significant because it was the first

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.330
time in the game's history where a map
that was already in the game would

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:32.280
receive a complete overall signaling
more of that to come in the future.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:37.020
Building the map from the ground up, the
new de_train was stunningly beautiful,

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:42.160
but valve included a critical mistake. It
would only take a couple of hours before

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.240
the community would learn of the bird boost.
With all the changes that Valve had made

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.620
to the map, a new addition was the spawning
of pigeons every couple of rounds down

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:54.729
by ivy. The big issue was that these
pigeons had hit boxes that players could

00:04:54.729 --> 00:04:59.530
use to jump on top of and then enter
the map with. The game breaking part of

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:03.520
this bug, was that, if used properly, it
would allow clear vision down into the

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.630
A bomb site for the player outside of
the map, giving them an extreme unfair

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:12.160
advantage. The bird boost was not
long-lived however as Valve quickly got

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.020
rid of the bug and set up this mural in
memory of this hilarious moment

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:24.360
Considered the worst

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.340
tournament ever held in the history of
CS:GO, 2015's Gaming Paradise was anything

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.820
but. To start things off, the tournament
itself began with a 12 hour delay in which

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:38.620
the tournament organizers explained the
delay was due to a road driver who

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.770
allegedly went missing with the
computers needed to run the tournament.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:44.650
Replacement computers were eventually
brought in, but they apparently were not

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:49.420
up to par and would drop frames and net
less than 100 frames per second in smokes.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:54.500
It gets worse. Police eventually arrived
at the players' hotels and confiscated

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.400
their passports because, as it turns out,
the organizers had not paid for those hotel

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.020
rooms. The passports were later returned
to the players once the police were able

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:08.110
to confirm that the rooms were in fact
booked by the organizers and not the

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.440
players or teams.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.380
The story continues with the tournament organizers
eventually drafting up a new contract

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:19.680
that stated that the players would be
receiving their prize money by

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:24.190
completing their games. But to no one's
surprise G2, who acquired the

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:27.790
Kinguin lineup that won the tournament,
released a statement later that year

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:31.660
which made it clear that the team was
not to receive any of the money that

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:37.340
they were promised. A fittingly
horrendous end to a terrible tournament.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:42.180
"And we found this new

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:46.920
site called CS:GO lotto." For better or
worse CS:GO and the gambling market has been

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.620
in an intricate love affair since skins
were first released in the arms deal

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:54.460
update back in 2013.
Many would even say that weapon skins

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.060
are the reason why the game is so
popular. Although it can be said that the

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:01.930
gambling scandals first began with
former CS:GO player and now popular

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:07.720
streamer Mohamad "m0E" Assad versus
CS:GO diamonds, the gambling scandals actually

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:11.260
really exploded when a Youtuber by the
name of Honour the Call made a video

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:16.030
providing evidence that popular Call of
Duty Youtubers TmarTn and ProSyndicate

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:20.950
have promoted and gambled on CS:GO lotto
without disclosing the fact that they

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:25.510
were owners of that website. Through this
story, several other notable streamers

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.789
and prominent personalities were dragged into the

00:07:27.789 --> 00:07:32.050
forefront and the community began to
seriously consider the legitimacy and

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:34.800
morality of such websites.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:37.520
Valve's response came in July, when it

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:41.199
released an announcement stating that
they had no business relationships with

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:44.590
any of the gambling websites and that it
would send out notices to cease

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:48.430
operations. Valve followed through shortly
after, with said letter and the letter

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:52.780
requested the immediate cease and desist
of a long list of popular gambling

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:57.610
websites such as CS:GO Lotto, CS:GO Wild,
CS:GO Diamonds, and most notably

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:01.900
CS:GO lounge. The gambling crackdown had
some stating that this was the end of

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:06.639
CS:GO's competitive scene and viewership,
but the CS:GO community held strong and

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.540
were able to set new viewership records
during the ELEAGUE Major.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:21.540
"l I don't even care, we're all on adderall. Like I don't even give a f--k."

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:25.419
CS:GO's doping scandal exploded mid
2015, when former Cloud9 member

00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:30.430
Semphis admitted that he and the entire
C9 lineup at ESL One Katowice were on

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:34.630
adderall. These remarks sparked a world
of controversy about the use of drugs in

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:39.010
esports and ESL responded quickly,
teaming up with the NADA to run their

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:42.940
first anti PED drug test for
ESL One: Cologne of that year. When

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:46.779
Cologne ended, ESL released an article
detailing the immense success of the

00:08:46.779 --> 00:08:51.040
event along with news that the random PED testing held during the event came

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.829
back negative, successfully putting an end to the

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.320
biggest drug scandal in CS:GO history.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:13.120
Near the end of 2014, a German pro player
SMN was first caught cheating through

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:17.390
the ESEA client. Valve and ESEA then
quickly worked together to help update the

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:22.160
Valve anti-cheat system and it was able
to detect and ban SMN of Team Alternate

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:27.120
Sf of Epsilon and most notably
Titan's very own KQLY.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:33.020
(Casting)

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.420
KQLY soon after the VAC ban came clean, stating that he had in fact used the cheat for a week.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:42.380
Following these VAC bans came an
extreme witch hunt. "I know that a couple

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:47.600
of my teammates are convinced that...they think they cheat." The man most affected

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:51.590
by this witch hunt was flusha of Fnatic.
As several videos accusing the Swede of

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:54.890
cheating became more and more popular
over the Internet. flusha defended his

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.650
stance stating that he had never
cheated and that he will never cheat

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:01.250
explaining that his unique playstyle and
tendency to lift his mouse a lot more

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:04.660
than other pros as some of the
explanations for the community suspicions.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:09.200
Although the witch hunt would die down
eventually as time past, speculations and

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.160
suspicions of pro players cheating still
continue to this day.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:27.140
A mistake that would change the lives of all the

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:30.860
members involved, the iBUYPOWER
match-fixing scandal is probably the

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:35.780
biggest esports scandal of all time. On
August 20th of 2014, two North American

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:39.650
teams iBUYPOWER
and NetcodeGuides.com were set to face

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:43.510
off in an online match for the fifth
season of CEVO's professional league.

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:49.460
iBUYPOWER were heavily favored in this
matchup, but were blown out 4-16. When

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:52.100
the match ended there was some
speculation that the match was thrown by

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:56.270
iBUYPOWER, but it was quickly brushed
aside as just rumors. The story then

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.690
later resurfaced when veteran
esports journalist Richard Lewis brought

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:04.290
to light new evidence in January of 2015. The new evidence provided by

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:08.250
Richard Lewis was incriminating text
messages by  Derek "dboorN" Boorn to his

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:13.500
former girlfriend. The text explicitly
said that quote they really did throw

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:17.130
that match and I bet for them on
alternate accounts end quote. The rest

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:21.330
is as they say history, as Valve put their
foot down on the people involved through

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:26.370
a blog post entitled integrity and fair
play in January of 2015. Although at the

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:30.330
time the bans were indefinite, Valve in
their 2016 blog post entitled "A

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:33.930
follow-up on integrity and fair play"
confirmed that the bans would be in

00:11:33.930 --> 00:11:35.740
fact permanent.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.640
Looking at the topic as a whole, we have to trace it back to

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:51.540
Canada's own Northern Arena where IMT's HEN1 was found not wearing his

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:55.230
headphones for the first two rounds of
their final map against Cloud9. This

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:58.230
event sparked a slew of controversy
around the importance of headgear

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:02.640
regulation and competitive play. Fast
forward about a month or so and sean

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.900
gares tweets out the following Tweet.
quote there needs to be rules on hazard

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:10.710
events without booths. You should never be
able to see a player's ear end quote.

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:13.950
This tweet stirred up a relevant
conversation about whether hats or

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:17.640
headgear in some way reduce the
effectiveness of noise canceling

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:21.870
headphones on the players heads. Although
no firm conclusion could be made about

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:25.740
whether headgear was in fact obtrusive
to the noise canceling headphones or

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:29.400
whether pro players could use that to an
advantage, the fact that the topic itself

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:33.750
was generating unnecessary controversy
around fair play became an issue. The

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:37.200
response was swift as ESL made an
official rule ahead of its pro league

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.130
finals that headgear such as beanies would
not be allowed to be worn. This trend

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:44.850
continued on to the recent ELEAGUE
Major qualifiers and even the ELEAGUE

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:48.480
Major itself and it will most likely
continue on for the foreseeable future.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:53.840
And that's the list. Please let us know
in the comments below if you felt like

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:57.390
we might have missed anything and
make sure to hit that like button and

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.280
subscribe for more CS:GO content.

